{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES REQUESTED at exact clean pushed head 4990f83bb52a0052fdb1946fdd0edbced5c346e9.\n\nARCHITECTURE. This changes Layer 3 resident/runtime composition and Layer 7 CLI/MCP surfaces; kernel validity, host binding, the Workroom fold, and projection contracts remain unchanged. Keeping the shared untrusted-advertisement clauses in internal/residentclient beside ValidateURL preserves dependency direction and is the right ownership, while each surface correctly owns its different recovery clause. The documentation does not yet match the exact behavior. docs/reference/gs/serve.md says an untrusted record stops every durable act until repair/removal, but gs --server - deliberately bypasses it. Its hard-killed-resident passage says durable acts refuse, while MCP transport loss still falls through to local AcceptSubmission. It also omits the adapter error's explicit third recovery, using gs --server - on purpose. Narrow those claims and say that residentclient owns the shared clause, not each complete surface sentence.\n\nSECURITY. The fresh-room path is sound: all durable MCP tools converge on submit; resolveEndpoint precedes workspace.Actor and BuildActRequest; app.ResidentAdvertisement bounds and separates five record failures; ValidateURL rejects the sixth before any off-loopback dial; and the new tests prove class-specific refusal, unchanged depth, read fallback, and session survival. A concrete fail-open transition blocks approval. resolveEndpoint reads ResidentAdvertisement only when baseURL is empty. After a valid endpoint is cached, a later unreadable, oversized, malformed, addressless, foreign-workroom, or non-loopback advertisement is ignored. If the cached resident is then unreachable, submit clears the cache after the transport error and falls through to workspace.AcceptSubmission, silently appending locally. I reproduced this with a production-shaped overlay test: a joined room cached a valid endpoint, the advertisement became malformed, the resident stopped, and state returned success; the assertion 'durable act was accepted after the advertisement became untrustworthy' failed. Make the durable path classify the current advertisement independently of the read/presence cache before private-key access, update the address coherently, and recheck before any transport-loss local fallback as needed. Add this transition for all six classes and prove unchanged depth while reads still fall back. Also add a mutation-sensitive key-order test using unusable advertisement plus unreadable/corrupt actor custody; moving resolution below workspace.Actor currently leaves every new test green.\n\nARTIFACTS. The head changes six stable paths: cmd/gitseq-mcp, cmd/gs, internal/residentclient, and three documentation pages. Only five artifacts were filed. cmd/gs/main.go changed but has no exact-head cmd/gs artifact, so the implementation report and future succession set are incomplete. Publish that artifact and replace the review request on the repaired exact head. Ensure documentation artifacts rest on all behavior artifacts they describe.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. The three-outcome resolver and the small discarding endpoint wrapper are an honest, compact model for reads. Do not remove cached-URL validation as a cosmetic simplification while repairing the durable/cache split; first make the current-advertisement rule explicit. Correcting all three pages that carried the old divergence was proper scope, not fluff.\n\nEVIDENCE. HEAD and origin/request/mcp-advert-durable-refusal both equal 4990f83b and the worktree is clean. Focused new MCP tests passed; full cmd/gitseq-mcp passed independently; internal/app resident-advertisement and internal/residentclient tests passed; cmd/gs advertisement/resident tests passed; internal/docset passed uncached; git diff --check passed. Those gates do not cover the fail-open transition or the artifact defect. Do not merge this head.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4b